# Template for .env.production.local — copy it and keep the copy out of Git:
#
#   cp .env.production.local.example .env.production.local
#
# Keep the `.local` suffix on the copy. `.env.production` (without it) sits
# BELOW .env.local in Next's load order, so a value there would lose to
# whatever a developer has set locally.
#
# ===== WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR =====
#
# Build-time env for every Cloudflare-targeted build — both `pnpm preview` and
# `pnpm deploy`. Each runs `opennextjs-cloudflare build`, which shells out to
# `next build`, and `next build` always resolves NODE_ENV=production. Next then
# loads the `.env.production.local` tier above .env.local, so values here
# override local-dev values for any built output while leaving `next dev` alone.
#
# That grouping is deliberate. The meaningful split isn't preview-vs-deploy,
# it's `next dev` (Node, real filesystem) vs. anything running under workerd
# (`wrangler dev` for preview, and the deployed Worker), which have no
# persistent writable filesystem. A driver that's wrong in production is
# equally wrong in preview, so both should build the same way.
#
# NEXTJS_ENV=development in .dev.vars does NOT exempt preview: that var is read
# by the `opennextjs-cloudflare preview`/`deploy` commands to resolve the
# Worker's own runtime env, and has no influence on the `next build` that
# already ran. next.config.ts is only ever evaluated by that build.
#
# ===== WHAT BELONGS HERE =====
#
# Only vars that next.config.ts reads at BUILD time, where wrangler.jsonc's
# `vars` can't reach — those populate the deployed Worker's runtime env, after
# the build is already done. Anything the app reads at request time belongs in
# wrangler.jsonc (non-sensitive, universal) or .secrets.production (sensitive
# or per-environment) instead.
#
# Nothing sensitive needs to go here today. If that changes, remember this
# file's contents are baked into the build output rather than injected as a
# Worker secret.

# Read by next.config.ts's turbopack.resolveAlias to decide whether to swap the
# Postgres driver for the `pg`-free stub. Must stay non-POSTGRES for the
# Cloudflare target: `pg` does an unconditional require("pg-cloudflare") that
# fails to resolve when bundled for workerd, and this alias is the only thing
# keeping `pg` out of the compiled output.
#
# Leaving this unset also works for now, since the alias applies to any value
# that isn't "POSTGRES". Set it explicitly anyway — an unset var silently
# depends on that fallback, and a developer with CREDENTIALS_STORE_DRIVER=
# POSTGRES in their own .env.local would otherwise pull `pg` into a Workers
# build and hit a resolve failure with no obvious cause.
#
# SQLITE is a placeholder that satisfies the build constraint. It is NOT a
# working runtime driver under workerd — the SQLite driver writes to a local
# file (CREDENTIALS_SQLITE_PATH), which neither `wrangler dev` nor a deployed
# Worker provides. It's inert today only because DATA_MODE=MOCK never reads a
# stored credential. This becomes the D1 driver once that exists.
#
# Keep in sync with CREDENTIALS_STORE_DRIVER in wrangler.jsonc's `vars`, which
# is what the deployed Worker reads at runtime. Nothing enforces that the two
# agree — if they drift you get a bundle built for one driver and a runtime
# asking for another.
CREDENTIALS_STORE_DRIVER=SQLITE
